/*!
\page qxmpp-guidelines.html
\title API Guidelines

Guidelines for code contributed to QXmpp. See also \c CONTRIBUTING.md for merge requests, tests
and copyright headers.

\section1 Naming of New APIs

New public APIs use the \c QXmpp namespace instead of the \c QXmpp class prefix.
The existing \c QXmpp* classes are renamed in QXmpp 2.0.

\list 1
\li Entry points are declared directly in \c QXmpp: the client, the managers, the storage
    interfaces and their implementations, the top-level stanzas and types shared by several
    features (e.g. \c QXmpp::OmemoManager, \c QXmpp::OmemoStorage, \c QXmpp::OmemoMemoryStorage,
    \c QXmpp::TrustLevel).
    A manager is always named \c QXmpp::<Feature>Manager, even if a namespace
    \c QXmpp::<Feature> exists.
\li The types of a single feature are declared in \c QXmpp::<Feature>, e.g. the types a manager
    returns, takes or emits (\c QXmpp::Omemo::Device, \c QXmpp::Omemo::DeviceWatch,
    \c QXmpp::Muc::Role).
    A feature without such types does not get a namespace.
\li Public namespaces are at most two levels deep (\c QXmpp::<Feature>).
    The name of a namespace must not be the name of a type in \c QXmpp.
\li A feature has the same name everywhere: in the namespace, in the manager and in the other
    entry points (\c QXmpp::Omemo, \c QXmpp::OmemoManager, \c QXmpp::OmemoStorage).
\li Internal code is declared in \c QXmpp::Private or \c QXmpp::<Feature>::Private; these rules
    do not apply to it.
\endlist

Signals, slots and properties (\c Q_PROPERTY) need fully qualified types for moc and QML.
Keeping the entry points in \c QXmpp keeps those types short.

\section1 Design of New APIs

\list
\li Hooks instead of virtual functions for extension points
\li QXmpp::Iq::Stanza with payload structs instead of QXmppIq subclasses
\li QXmppTask<QXmpp::Result<T>> instead of signals for results
\li QBindable for observable state
\li QXmpp::Jid instead of QString
\li \c u""_s for string literals, QStringView for string parameters that are only read
\endlist

Hooks are used where the handlers take part in the processing: they return a result, modify the
arguments, stop further handlers or are awaited. Extensions also notify each other via hooks,
which needs no common header. Notifications for applications that do not influence the
processing are signals, or QBindable properties for state.

\section1 Compatibility

QXmpp is source compatible between minor versions, but not binary compatible: the ABI changes
with every minor version.

Public APIs are deprecated before they are removed. Only the declarations in the public headers
are guarded by \c{#if QXMPP_DEPRECATED_SINCE(major, minor)}; the implementations in the \c .cpp
files are always built. Where QXmpp uses a deprecated API itself, the warning is disabled via
\c QT_WARNING_DISABLE_DEPRECATED.

\section1 Documentation and Comments

New public APIs are documented with \c{\since QXmpp X.Y}.

Comments explain what cannot be seen in the code, e.g. why something is done. They do not repeat
the code or the commit message.

\section1 Containers

Maps are \c std::unordered_map, in internal code and in new public APIs, not QHash. Unlike QHash,
it takes move-only values (e.g. QXmppPromise), keeps references to its elements valid on
insertion and never detaches. A \c std::hash specialization is only needed for custom keys; Qt
provides it for QString and QByteArray.

Lists in public APIs are QList: applications and QML use it, returning a stored list is cheap
thanks to implicit sharing and a \c std::vector would have to be converted. \c std::vector is
used where the elements are move-only.

\section1 Internal Helpers

Before writing a helper, look for an existing one, mainly in the internal headers in \c src/base:
\c Async.h (e.g. \c AttachableRequests for sharing a running request between all callers
requesting the same data), \c Algorithms.h, \c QXmppUtils_p.h, \c Enums.h and \c XmlWriter.h.
If a helper does almost what is needed, extend it instead of adding a variant next to it.

Enums are parsed and serialized via \c Enums.h: a specialization of \c Enums::Data lists the
string of each value, \c Enums::fromString() and \c Enums::toString() convert them and
\c XmlWriter writes such enums directly. Do not write \c switch statements or string tables for
this.
*/
